--- a/v1/kv_offload/transfer_log.py
+++ b/v1/kv_offload/transfer_log.py
@@ -11,10 +11,18 @@
 This module emits one INFO line per transfer, interspersed in the normal log:
 
     KV LOAD  chatcmpl-a9b273614cd4c815 | fs   -> RAM  |  44 blk
-    KV LOAD  chatcmpl-a9b273614cd4c815 | RAM  -> VRAM |  44 blk /  36608 tok (GPU had 50752 tok)
-    KV STORE chatcmpl-b88083b7827488ae | VRAM -> RAM  |  12 blk /   9984 tok
+    KV LOAD  chatcmpl-a9b273614cd4c815 | RAM  -> VRAM |  44 blk /  36608 tok (had 50752 tok, now 87360 tok in VRAM)
+    KV STORE chatcmpl-b88083b7827488ae | VRAM -> RAM  |  12 blk /   9984 tok (now 39936 tok backed up in RAM, 39936 tok in VRAM)
     KV STORE chatcmpl-b88083b7827488ae | RAM  -> fs   |  12 blk
 
+A STORE is a copy, not an eviction -- the source blocks stay resident in
+VRAM (that's what makes them storable at all), it only gives them a
+backup on a lower tier. The "now ... tok" figures track that: the RAM
+figure is this request's actual backed-up-so-far boundary (the minimum
+across KV-cache groups, since a hybrid model's groups can progress at
+different rates -- the same reasoning the HIT_DIVERGED rescue uses
+elsewhere), and the VRAM figure never drops on a STORE line.
+
 A full disk-backed resume therefore reads top to bottom as
 ``fs -> RAM`` then ``RAM -> VRAM``, which is the staged promotion the tiering
 manager actually performs (secondary tiers cannot touch GPU memory directly).
